Audits often require thorough paperwork reviews, which can create backlogs even before potential phishing campaigns begin. During these lulls in activity, users might become more relaxed since there aren't immediate threats around every corner - giving scammers ample opportunity to strike when defenses are down!

Additionally, genuine inspections usually follow set processes and procedures designed specifically for accuracy and effectiveness rather than efficiency alone. This means auditors double check information against records multiple times throughout evaluation stages rather than simply relying upon initial reports submitted by employees themselves (who may inadvertently include incorrect details due lackadaisical approach towards data entry). On top board this extra layer precaution helps reduce instances where legitimate requests go unnoticed because people grew accustomed regular routine but still vigilant enough recognize red flags posed unfamiliar email addresses asking sensitive material normally kept private like financial accounts etc...

Overall understanding how audit cycles work alongside typical employee behavior under normal circumstances makes clear what kind tactics need deployed successfully pull off convincing ruse despite obvious irregularities involved.

